remonial**.\n  - Presenters and recipients are dressed in formal attire (tuxedos and evening gowns).\n  - The presenter typically approaches the recipient, shakes hands, and then hands over the award.\n  - Recipients often bow or nod in acknowledgment (as seen in Frame 3).\n  - The handover is deliberate and respectful, with both parties facing each other and maintaining eye contact.\n\nIn summary, the award presentation is a traditional, in-person ceremony with physical handovers of medals and certificates, conducted in a formal setting with no indication of remote participation. The split-screen is a production choice for broadcasting, not a sign of virtual attendance.\n'], 'condensed_response': '**Condensed scene summary (character‑by‑character, frame‑by‑frame)**  \n\n| Frame (file) | Main characters & attire | Action & interaction | Key visual cue |\n|--------------|--------------------------|----------------------|----------------|\n| **frame_0755.jpg** (0\u202fs) | Audience, stage background | Wide‑shot of the ceremony hall – people seated, bright stage lights. | Sets the formal, in‑person setting. |\n| **frame_1815.jpg** (≈\u202f2\u202fs) | **Presenter** (black tail‑coat) & **Recipient\u202f1** (tuxedo) | Presenter approaches, shakes hands with Recipient\u202f1. | First physical hand‑off; confirms on‑stage participation. |\n| **frame_3186.jpg** (≈\u202f4\u202fs) | **Recipient\u202f1** (still in tuxedo) | Recipient bows slightly, holding a red‑covered certificate/folder. | Shows respect and receipt of award material. |\n| **frame_1879.jpg** (≈\u202f6\u202fs) | **Presenter** (same) & **Recipient\u202f2** (gown) | Presenter extends a medal on a ribbon and a certificate/envelope to Recipient\u202f2, who accepts with both hands. | Direct physical hand‑over of medal\u202f+\u202fcertificate. |\n| **frame_0731.jpg** (≈\u202f8\u202fs) | **Presenter** (off‑stage) & **Speaker** (in a side room) | Split‑screen: left panel continues main stage; right panel shows a speaker at a podium beside a statue and greenery. Both are physically present in the venue. | Split‑screen is a broadcast technique, not remote participation. |\n| **frame_0764.jpg** (≈\u202f10\u202fs) | **Recipient\u202f2** (close‑up) | Holds an open, colorful certificate and a small box (likely the medal) up for the camera. | Confirms the items being awarded are medals and certificates. |\n\n**Overall narrative**\n\n- The ceremony is a **traditional, in‑person award presentation** on a large stage, streamed with a split‑screen broadcast to show multiple angles.  \n- **Presenters** (uniform black tail‑coats) **approach each recipient**, exchange a formal handshake, then **hand over a medal and a certificate**.  \n- **Recipients** (tuxedos or gowns) receive the awards with both hands, often bowing or nodding, maintaining eye‑contact with the presenter.  \n- No video screens, phones, or other remote‑participation gear appear; all participants are physically present in the same venue.  \n\nThus, the scene is a formal, fully onsite award hand‑over, using broadcast split‑screens solely for visual variety.', 'failed_images': []}}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '{\n    "tool": "RECORD",\n    "entries": [\n        "Time: 755 seconds, Event: Three people stand on stage holding their awards while a fourth person presents them",\n        "Time: 1815 seconds, Event: Man in black tuxedo hands Nobel Prize medal to another man on blue stage",\n        "Time: 3186 seconds, Event: Man in black tuxedo bows head while receiving medal from another man on stage",\n        "Time: 1879 seconds, Event: Man hands blue envelope to elderly man in black tuxedo on stage with podium",\n        "Time: 731 seconds, Event: Man and woman shown receiving award, likely Nobel Prize, in formal event",\n        "Time: 764 seconds, Event: Person holding framed certificate and gold medal in box while man stands at podium"\n    ]\n}'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Successfully recorded 6 event(s). You now have 6 total recorded events. Use VIEW_RECORDS to see all of them sorted by time.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '{\n    "tool": "FINAL_ANSWER",\n    "frames": [\n        "frames/frame_0755.jpg",\n        "frames/frame_1815.jpg",\n        "frames/frame_3186.jpg",\n        "frames/frame_1879.jpg",\n        "frames/frame_0731.jpg",\n        "frames/frame_0764.jpg"\n    ],\n    "answer": "2",\n    "reasoning": "Based on the VLM analysis of multiple key frames showing the award presentation, the Nobel Peace Prize is presented through face-to-face, in-person ceremonies with physical handovers of medals and certificates. The evidence shows presenters physically handing awards to recipients on stage, shaking hands, and recipients bowing in acceptance. There is no evidence of remote participation via telephone, video links, or broadcasting equipment for the actual award presentations. The split-screen format observed is a production technique for broadcasting, not a method of remote award presentation.",\n    "answer_criteria": [\n        "Physical handovers of medals and certificates occur on stage between presenters and recipients",\n        "No video screens, telephone equipment, or remote participation technology is visible during award presentations",\n        "All interactions show direct face-to-face contact between presenters and award recipients"\n    ]\n}'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': '📋 CRITIC FEEDBACK (Confidence: 33%)'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '{\n    "tool": "VLM_QUERY",\n    "frames": ["frames/frame_0726.jpg", "frames/frame_0727.jpg", "frames/frame_0728.jpg", "frames/frame_0729.jpg", "frames/frame_0730.jpg", "frames/frame_0731.jpg", "frames/frame_0732.jpg", "frames/frame_0733.jpg", "frames/frame_0734.jpg", "frames/frame_0735.jpg", "frames/frame_0736.jpg", "frames/frame_0750.jpg", "frames/frame_0751.jpg", "frames/frame_0752.jpg", "frames/frame_0753.jpg", "frames/frame_0754.jpg", "frames/frame_0755.jpg", "frames/frame_0756.jpg", "frames/frame_0757.jpg", "frames/frame_0758.jpg", "frames/frame_0759.jpg", "frames/frame_0760.jpg", "frames/frame_0761.jpg", "frames/frame_0762.jpg", "frames/frame_0763.jpg", "frames/frame_0764.jpg", "frames/frame_0765.jpg", "frames/frame_0766.jpg", "frames/frame_0767.jpg", "frames/frame_0768.jpg", "frames/frame_0769.jpg", "frames/frame_1810.jpg", "frames/frame_1811.jpg", "frames/frame_1812.jpg", "frames/frame_1813.jpg", "frames/frame_1814.jpg", "frames/frame_1815.jpg", "frames/frame_1816.jpg", "frames/frame_1817.jpg", "frames/frame_1818.jpg", "frames/frame_1819.jpg", "frames/frame_1820.jpg", "frames/frame_1874.jpg", "frames/frame_1875.jpg", "frames/frame_1876.jpg", "frames/frame_1877.jpg", "frames/frame_1878.jpg", "frames/frame_1879.jpg", "frames/frame_1880.jpg", "frames/frame_1881.jpg", "frames/frame_1882.jpg", "frames/frame_1883.jpg", "frames/frame_1884.jpg", "frames/frame_3181.jpg", "frames/frame_3182.jpg", "frames/frame_3183.jpg", "frames/frame_3184.jpg", "frames/frame_3185.jpg", "frames/frame_3186.jpg", "frames/frame_3187.jpg", "frames/frame_3188.jpg", "frames/frame_3189.jpg", "frames/frame_3190.jpg", "frames/frame_3191.jpg"],\n    "prompt": "Analyze these frames to determine the format of the Nobel Peace Prize award presentation. Check for: 1) Award presentation method (in-person, video call, telephone, broadcast) 2) Physical presence of award recipients on stage 3) Use of video screens or telecommunication equipment 4) Interaction between presenter and recipient 5) Method of handing over award (physical object vs virtual) 6) Audience reaction to presentation format. Verify if answer \'2\' (Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards) is correct by examining whether awards are presented through broadcasting/connecting methods rather than in-person, telephone, or video link formats."\n}'}, {'role': 'vlm response', 'content': {'batched_responses': [{'batch_start': 0, 'batch_end': 30, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0726.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0727.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0728.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0729.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0730.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0731.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0732.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0733.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0734.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0735.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0736.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0750.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0751.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0752.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0753.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0754.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0755.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0756.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0757.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0758.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0759.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0760.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0761.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0762.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0763.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0764.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0765.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0766.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0767.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0768.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on the visual evidence across the 30 frames, here is a detailed analysis of the Nobel Peace Prize award presentation format:\n\n---\n\n**1) Award presentation method (in-person, video call, telephone, broadcast)**  \n✅ **Broadcasting and connecting awards** — The presentation is clearly being broadcast live or recorded for broadcast. The split-screen format shows two distinct physical locations connected via video feed: one showing the main stage with the presenter and audience, and the other showing recipients receiving their awards in a separate location. This is not a simple video call or telephone; it is a professionally produced broadcast with multiple camera angles and seamless switching between locations.\n\n---\n\n**2) Physical presence of award recipients on stage**  \n❌ **No** — The award recipients are **not physically present on the main stage** with the presenter. They appear in a separate video feed (left panel), often in a different room or venue, receiving the award from a local representative. The main stage (right panel) features only the presenter and audience.\n\n---\n\n**3) Use of video screens or telecommunication equipment**  \n✅ **Yes** — The entire presentation relies on video screens and telecommunication equipment. The split-screen layout is a clear indicator that the event is being broadcast and connected across locations. The recipients are shown via video feed, and their interactions are mediated through this technology.\n\n---\n\n**4) Interaction between presenter and recipient**  \n✅ **Indirect, mediated interaction** — There is no direct physical interaction. The presenter (on the right screen) speaks, while the recipients (on the left screen) listen and respond. Their interaction is synchronized via broadcast, not real-time video call or in-person conversation. The presenter does not hand the award directly; instead, a local representative does so in the recipient’s location.\n\n---\n\n**5) Method of handing over award (physical object vs virtual)**  \n✅ **Physical object** — The award (diploma and medal) is handed over as a **physical object**. In frames 4–7 and 14–19, we see a local representative physically handing the diploma and medal to the recipient in their location. The award is not virtual or symbolic; it is tangible and presented in person — just not by the main presenter on the main stage.\n\n---\n\n**6) Audience reaction to presentation format**  \n✅ **Audience is present and engaged** — In frames 2, 3, 8–11, and 12–13, the audience in the main hall is shown applauding and reacting to the presentation. Their presence and applause indicate acceptance and normalization of the hybrid broadcast format. There is no visible confusion or negative reaction — the format appears to be standard and expected.\n\n---\n\n**Verification of Answer ‘2’: “Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards”**\n\n✅ **Correct.**  \nThe evidence overwhelmingly supports that the presentation **adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards**. The format is not in-person (recipients are not on the main stage), not telephone (no audio-only interaction), and not a simple video call (it’s a multi-camera, professionally produced broadcast with split-screen feeds connecting multiple physical locations). The awards are physically handed over in the recipients’ local venues, while the ceremony is broadcast to a global audience and synchronized with the main stage.\n\nThis hybrid broadcast model allows for simultaneous recognition across locations while maintaining the ceremonial gravity of the event.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**  \n✅ The answer **“2) Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards”** is **correct** and fully supported by the visual evidence.'}, {'batch_start': 30, 'batch_end': 60, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0769.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1810.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1811.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1812.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1813.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1814.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1815.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1816.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1817.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1818.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1819.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1820.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1874.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1875.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1876.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1877.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1878.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1879.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1880.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1881.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1882.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1883.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_1884.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3181.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3182.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3183.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3184.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3185.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3186.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3187.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on a detailed analysis of the 30 provided video frames, here is the assessment of the Nobel Peace Prize award presentation format:\n\n---\n\n**1) Award presentation method (in-person, video call, telephone, broadcast)**  \n→ **In-person.** All interactions occur physically on stage. The presenter and recipients are in the same room, standing face-to-face. No evidence of video call, telephone, or remote broadcast is present during the actual award handover.\n\n**2) Physical presence of award recipients on stage**  \n→ **Yes.** Recipients are physically present on stage, walking to the podium, receiving the award, shaking hands, and bowing. For example, Frame 5 shows a recipient walking toward the presenter, and Frame 8–11 show direct physical interaction.\n\n**3) Use of video screens or telecommunication equipment**  \n→ **Minimal and contextual.** Frame 1 shows a split-screen format, likely for broadcast or archival purposes, showing two different scenes simultaneously — one with presenters holding certificates and another with a recipient at a podium. However, this appears to be a production choice for viewers, not a method of award delivery. No telecommunication equipment (e.g., microphones for remote speakers, video feeds of absent recipients) is used to conduct the award ceremony itself.\n\n**4) Interaction between presenter and recipient**  \n→ **Direct, physical, and ceremonial.** The presenter shakes hands, hands over a physical diploma and medal, and exchanges bows or nods. This is visible in Frames 8–11, 17–22, and 25–27.\n\n**5) Method of handing over award (physical object vs virtual)**  \n→ **Physical object.** The award consists of a diploma and medal, both physically handed over. In Frame 9, the recipient receives a folded document (diploma) and a medal is visible around his neck in later frames (e.g., Frame 13).\n\n**6) Audience reaction to presentation format**  \n→ **Positive and formal.** The audience, dressed in formal attire, applauds after each award is presented (e.g., Frame 22, 27, 28). Their reaction is consistent with a traditional, in-person ceremony — no surprise or adjustment to virtual formats is observed.\n\n---\n\n**Verification of Answer ‘2’ (Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards):**\n\nThe phrase “broadcasting and connecting awards” is ambiguous but appears to suggest that the awards were delivered via broadcast (i.e., remotely or virtually connected). **This is incorrect based on the evidence.**\n\n- While the event is *broadcast* (as most Nobel ceremonies are), the *presentation itself* is **in-person**.\n- The “connecting” aspect (if interpreted as connecting remotely via video/telecom) is **not** used for the actual award handover.\n- The split-screen in Frame 1 may reflect a broadcast edit or simultaneous coverage, but it does not indicate that the award was *delivered* remotely.\n\n**Conclusion: Answer ‘2’ is NOT correct.** The ceremony clearly follows an **in-person** format for award presentation, with physical handover of objects and direct interaction. The use of broadcasting is for audience dissemination, not for connecting absent recipients.\n\n---\n\n✅ Final Answer: **The Nobel Peace Prize award presentation in these frames was conducted in-person, with physical presence of recipients, direct interaction, and handover of physical awards. The format does not match “broadcasting and connecting awards” as described in answer ‘2’.**'}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 64, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3188.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3189.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3190.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3191.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on the visual evidence from the four frames provided, here is the analysis of the Nobel Peace Prize award presentation format:\n\n---\n\n**1) Award presentation method:**  \n✅ **In-person** — The frames show a formal ceremony taking place on a physical stage in a large auditorium. The presenter and recipient are both physically present on stage, interacting directly.\n\n**2) Physical presence of award recipients on stage:**  \n✅ **Yes** — The recipient is clearly standing on stage, receiving the award from the presenter. In Frame 1, the recipient (a woman) is seen accepting the award from a man in formal attire (likely the presenter or Nobel Committee representative).\n\n**3) Use of video screens or telecommunication equipment:**  \n❌ **No visible use** — There are no video screens, monitors, or telecommunication devices (e.g., microphones for remote connection, video call setups) indicating remote participation. The ceremony appears entirely live and in-person.\n\n**4) Interaction between presenter and recipient:**  \n✅ **Direct, face-to-face interaction** — In Frame 1, the presenter is handing the award to the recipient with a handshake or gesture of presentation. In Frames 2–3, the presenter bows slightly, indicating a formal, personal exchange.\n\n**5) Method of handing over award:**  \n✅ **Physical object** — The award (likely a medal and diploma) is handed over as a tangible item. The recipient is seen holding it in Frame 1.\n\n**6) Audience reaction to presentation format:**  \n✅ **Audience is physically present and applauding** — Frame 4 shows a large, live audience seated in the auditorium, clapping and observing the ceremony. There is no indication of virtual or remote audience participation.\n\n---\n\n**Verification of Answer ‘2’ (Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards):**\n\n❌ **Incorrect.**  \nThe phrase “broadcasting and connecting awards” implies a remote or mediated format — such as live broadcast with virtual connections, video calls, or teleconferencing — which is **not** what is depicted.\n\nThe frames clearly show:\n- A **live, in-person ceremony**.\n- **No remote participants**.\n- **No video links or telecommunication equipment** in use.\n- **Direct physical handover** of the award.\n\nThus, the format is **not** “broadcasting and connecting awards” — it is a **traditional in-person award ceremony**.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Final Conclusion:**  \nAnswer ‘2’ is **incorrect**. The Nobel Peace Prize in these frames is presented **in-person**, not via broadcasting or remote connection methods. The correct format is **physical, live, and face-to-face**.'}], 'formatted_responses': ['\n=== Batch 0-30 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0726.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0727.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0728.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0729.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0730.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0731.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0732.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0733.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0734.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0735.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0736.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0750.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0751.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0752.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0753.jpg, /m